# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
boethius-2.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: boethius-2.logic.r2 has "office_signalizes_iniquity → position_brings_infamy"; boethius-2.logic.r2.2 and boethius-2.logic.r2.3 have "position_brings_infamy"; they differ when nothing is true, which the first allows
boethius-2.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps boethius-2.logic.r2.2 and boethius-2.logic.r2.3, to: Suppose "offices are wont to signalize iniquity rather than chase it away" and "high position brings infamy upon the bad" are false. Is S5, "Dost not see what infamy high position brings upon the bad?", true then?
boethius-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: what the text leaves unstated is read 2 ways: boethius-2.logic.r2 has "honours_fall_to_wicked ∧ ¬office_plants_virtue → unworthy_of_office"; boethius-2.logic.r2.2 and boethius-2.logic.r2.3 have "honours_fall_to_wicked → unworthy_of_office" and "office_honours → revered_for_office"; they differ when only "official dignity clothes him to whom it comes with honour and reverence", "offices of state have power to plant virtue in the minds of their possessors and drive out vice", "honours often fall to the most iniquitous of men" and "the holders are men we deem unworthy of the office itself" are true, which the first allows
boethius-2.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: boethius-2.logic.r2 has "¬revered_for_office"; boethius-2.logic.r2.2 and boethius-2.logic.r2.3 have "¬office_honours"; they differ when only "official dignity clothes him to whom it comes with honour and reverence" is true, which the first allows
boethius-2.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered yes, which keeps boethius-2.logic.r2, to: Suppose "official dignity clothes him to whom it comes with honour and reverence" is true and "we can deem such men worthy of reverence on account of their office" is false. Is the conclusion, "No; we cannot deem men worthy of reverence on account of their office, whom we deem unworthy of the office itself.", true then?
boethius-2.logic.r2: argue/readings: U1: boethius-2.logic.r2.2 and boethius-2.logic.r2.3 take for granted "if official dignity clothes him to whom it comes with honour and reverence, then we can deem such men worthy of reverence on account of their office"; boethius-2.logic.r2 does not
boethius-2.logic.r2: argue/answered: U1: answered yes, which keeps boethius-2.logic.r2.2 and boethius-2.logic.r2.3, to: Does the text's argument take this for granted, whether or not it says so: "if official dignity clothes him to whom it comes with honour and reverence, then we can deem such men worthy of reverence on account of their office"? Answer yes if the author would accept it as part of the argument.
